        "India adopted anti-dumping legislation in 1985 but made little use of it until the trade liberalisation of the 1990s sharply reduced applied tariffs. As tariffs on intermediate goods fell from over 80 percent in the early 1990s to around 10 percent by the late 2000s, domestic producers turned increasingly to anti-dumping petitions, and the number of investigations rose rapidly [23]. Bown and Tovar {23} show that products that experienced larger tariff cuts in the 1990s were more likely to be protected subsequently through anti-dumping or safeguard measures, suggesting that trade remedies partly reversed the liberalisation.",
        "Investigations are conducted by the Directorate General of Anti-Dumping and Allied Duties (renamed the Directorate General of Trade Remedies in 2018) under the Ministry of Commerce. A petition by producers accounting for a substantial share of domestic output triggers an initiation, after which the authority examines whether imports were dumped and whether they caused injury. Preliminary findings may lead to provisional duties; the final findings, usually issued within twelve to eighteen months of initiation, recommend definitive duties, which the Ministry of Finance then imposes. Duties are often specified as a fixed amount per tonne or as the difference between a reference price and the import price, and remain in force for five years unless extended after a sunset review.",
