        "A large literature studies the causes and effects of anti-dumping protection [2][3][12]. Studies of the protected industries find that duties raise prices and domestic output but have modest and often temporary effects on productivity: Konings and Vandenbussche {8} find productivity gains among European firms far from the technology frontier, and Pierce {9} shows that apparent productivity gains among US plants reflect higher prices rather than physical efficiency. Studies of targeted exporters document trade destruction and deflection to other markets [10][11]. Erbahar and Zi {14} show that US anti-dumping duties on inputs lead downstream industries to seek protection in turn, a phenomenon they call cascading protection, and Vandenbussche and Viegelahn {13} find that Indian firms reallocate inputs within the firm in response to anti-dumping duties on inputs.",
